  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-2 mb-4">Where we are and what we run</h2>
  <p>
    We are in Chenghai, Guangdong — the toy-manufacturing cluster in eastern Guangdong where a large share of the world's
    plastic toys are moulded, assembled and packed. The factory has been operating since 2001 and covers roughly 5,000
    square metres, with moulding, assembly, electronics sub-assembly and packing under one roof.
  </p>
  <p>
    That matters commercially for one reason: the carton data on this site comes from our own export records, not from a
    trading company's estimate. When we say a carton is 56.5 × 50 × 32 cm and holds 36 pieces, that is the carton we ship,
    and it is the number your forwarder will bill against.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-10 mb-4">The order, step by step</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>1. Enquiry and quotation.</strong> You send item numbers or a description and a target quantity. We reply with
    unit price, MOQ, carton data, CBM and an estimated production window. If the item is not a fit we will say so.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>2. Sample.</strong> A stock item ships in a few days; a customised item takes longer because the artwork or the
    colour change has to be produced. The sample is the document that everything afterwards is measured against.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>3. Approval and deposit.</strong> Once the sample is approved in writing, production is scheduled against the
    deposit. Nothing is produced before both of those exist.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>4. Production.</strong> Typically 15 to 25 days for a standard order, longer for a custom box or a new colour.
    If something changes in production, you hear it from us before it becomes a problem at inspection.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>5. Pre-shipment inspection.</strong> Appearance, function, packing and labelling, checked against the approved
    sample and the carton plan. This is the last point at which a defect is cheap to fix.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>6. Shipping documents and balance.</strong> Commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading or airway bill,
    and the conformity documents for the destination. Balance against the documents, then the goods move.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-10 mb-4">What we will not claim</h2>
  <p>
    We will not tell you every product in the catalogue is certified for every market. Testing scope varies by model and
    by market, and a claim on a website is not a document at a border. If you need a specific standard for a specific
    item, ask for the item number and the market, and we will tell you what exists for that item today.
  </p>
  <p>
    We also will not describe a product as food-grade, or claim a certificate we do not hold. If a buyer needs a claim we
    cannot support, the honest answer is that we cannot support it — which is more useful to you before the order than
    after the shipment.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-10 mb-4">Samples, in plain terms</h2>
  <p>
    Sample cost is the sample plus the freight, and the buyer pays the freight. If the first order reaches five cartons,
    the sample fee is refunded against it. Customised samples take longer and cost more because a colour change or a
    printed box is a small production run in its own right.
  </p>
  <p>
    We would rather send one sample that is right than three that are approximate. Tell us what the sample has to prove —
    the colour match, the bubble output, the case strength, the battery life — and we will send the item that can actually
    demonstrate it.
  </p>

